8
00:00:21.444 --> 00:00:31.129
The security backbone of the grocery platform, how customers, riders and staff prove who they are, what they're allowed to do, and how their data is protected.

9
00:00:31.409 --> 00:00:34.865
All four labs run real crypto and a real KMS.

10
00:00:35.145 --> 00:00:35.977
L1:

11
00:00:36.257 --> 00:00:38.028
JWT validation.

12
00:00:38.308 --> 00:00:49.999
A naive validator accepted 7 of 9 forged or invalid tokens (alg:none, algorithm confusion, a key embedded in the header, an expired token, wrong audience).

13
00:00:50.279 --> 00:00:53.884
A hardened validator accepted only the one valid token.

14
00:00:54.164 --> 00:01:05.001
Plus JWKS key rotation and refresh-token reuse detection: a stolen refresh token bought an attacker 43,200 minutes versus 15.

15
00:01:06.351 --> 00:01:07.759
L2: login.

16
00:01:08.039 --> 00:01:28.242
A control-conformance harness over real HTTP shows the hardened OTP endpoint locking after 5 wrong codes (versus unlimited) and the OAuth flow enforcing PKCE, registered redirect URIs, and single-use codes where the naive one issued tokens to every malformed request.

17
00:01:28.522 --> 00:01:30.229
L3: authorization.

18
00:01:30.509 --> 00:01:33.943
The same 12 requests through RBAC, ABAC and ReBAC.

19
00:01:34.223 --> 00:01:43.951
RBAC alone allowed the IDOR read, a cross-store edit, an over-limit refund and a no-purpose PII read; ABAC and ReBAC denied them.

20
00:01:44.231 --> 00:01:49.394
A vulnerable handler that checks the role but not the object leaks other customers' orders.

21
00:01:50.744 --> 00:01:52.195
L4: encryption.

22
00:01:52.475 --> 00:01:55.760
Envelope encryption with OpenBao (a real KMS):

23
00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:03.400
AES-256-GCM per record, DEKs wrapped by a KEK that never leaves the KMS.

24
00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:16.821
Tamper detection, KEK rotation that rewraps kilobytes not terabytes, crypto-shredding for delete my data, and DEK caching that was 115x cheaper than a KMS call per record.

25
00:02:17.101 --> 00:02:18.083
Reading time:

26
00:02:18.363 --> 00:02:20.325
120 min + code.

27
00:02:21.675 --> 00:02:22.529
Part 1.

28
00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:24.729
Introduction and Why It Matters.

29
00:02:25.979 --> 00:02:27.685
A story: five ways in.

30
00:02:27.965 --> 00:02:37.096
A security review of the grocery platform, a month before a funding round's due diligence, found five issues that each map to a control in this chapter:

31
00:02:37.376 --> 00:02:40.427
The refunds tool trusted the JWT's header.

32
00:02:40.707 --> 00:02:50.691
An engineer's hand-rolled validator read the alg field from the token and used it, so a token with alg: none (no signature) and role: admin was accepted.

33
00:02:50.971 --> 00:02:55.920
L1 reproduces this and four more forgeries a naive validator waves through.

34
00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.760
The OTP login had no attempt limit.

35
00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:02.560
A six-digit code with unlimited guesses is not a secret.

36
00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:07.619
L2 shows the difference between unlimited attempts and a lockout after five.

37
00:03:08.969 --> 00:03:16.585
A customer could read another customer's order by changing the ID in the URL (/orders or o-99).

38
00:03:16.865 --> 00:03:26.443
The handler checked that the caller was logged in, not that the order was theirs, the classic IDOR (OWASP API 1, Broken Object Level Authorization).

39
00:03:26.723 --> 00:03:31.587
L3 shows a role-only check leaking it, and an object-level check stopping it.

40
00:03:31.867 --> 00:03:34.854
Store managers could refund any amount, in any store.

41
00:03:35.134 --> 00:03:40.681
Roles said manager can refund; they couldn't say up to 5,000, rupeesin your own store.

42
00:03:40.961 --> 00:03:45.377
L3 contrasts RBAC with attribute- and relationship-based models.

43
00:03:46.727 --> 00:03:54.513
Customer addresses and phone numbers were stored in plaintext, and delete my account left copies in backups for 35 days.

44
00:03:54.793 --> 00:03:58.015
L4 builds envelope encryption and crypto-shredding.

45
00:03:58.295 --> 00:04:00.791
None of these needed a sophisticated attacker.

46
00:04:01.071 --> 00:04:11.140
They're the routine failures that the OWASP API Top 10, the OAuth 2.0 Security BCP and basic key management exist to prevent.

47
00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:13.173
Quote:

48
00:04:13.453 --> 00:04:15.117
The thesis of this chapter.

49
00:04:15.397 --> 00:04:20.687
Security is not a feature you add; it's a set of defaults that fail closed at every layer.

50
00:04:20.967 --> 00:04:28.114
Authentication proves who; authorization decides what, and must be checked on every object, not just at the door.

51
00:04:28.394 --> 00:04:32.383
Secrets and keys live in a KMS, never in code or config.

52
00:04:32.663 --> 00:04:37.058
Data is encrypted so that losing the storage doesn't mean losing the data.

53
00:04:37.338 --> 00:04:45.914
And every one of these is testable, the labs here are the kind of conformance checks that belong in CI, because we think it's secure is not evidence.

54
00:04:46.194 --> 00:04:49.586
A note on this chapter: it teaches how to build the defenses.

55
00:04:49.866 --> 00:04:54.026
Every lab hardens a control and verifies bad inputs are refused.

56
00:04:54.306 --> 00:04:56.119
It does not provide attack tooling.

