00:07:26.855 --> 00:07:27.730
Part 2.

114
00:07:28.010 --> 00:07:29.909
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

115
00:07:31.159 --> 00:07:32.076
Intuition.

116
00:07:32.356 --> 00:07:38.201
Authentication is the ID check at the door; authorization is what your wristband lets you into.

117
00:07:38.481 --> 00:07:48.039
Being inside the building (logged in) doesn't mean you may enter the cash room (an admin action) or open someone else's locker (another customer's order).

118
00:07:48.319 --> 00:07:53.268
The IDOR bug is a guard who checks you have a wristband but never which locker is yours.

119
00:07:53.548 --> 00:07:56.492
A JWT is a tamper-evident wristband.

120
00:07:56.772 --> 00:08:00.228
It carries claims (who, what role, until when) and a signature.

121
00:08:00.508 --> 00:08:09.105
Trusting the writing on the band about how to check the signature (this band says to verify it with no ink at all) is the alg-confusion class of bugs.

122
00:08:10.455 --> 00:08:15.191
A KMS is the hotel's key-cutting machine that never leaves the back office.

123
00:08:15.471 --> 00:08:22.469
You send it a locked box (a wrapped data key) and it hands back the contents, but the master die never comes out front.

124
00:08:22.749 --> 00:08:28.253
Destroy a guest's die and every box cut from it is permanently locked, that's crypto-shredding.

125
00:08:28.533 --> 00:08:33.717
Zero trust is show your badge at every door, not you're past reception, so wander freely.

126
00:08:33.997 --> 00:08:42.039
On a network, being inside the perimeter grants nothing; each service still proves its identity (mTLS) and checks permissions.

127
00:08:43.389 --> 00:08:45.885
Authentication versus authorization.

128
00:08:46.165 --> 00:08:47.531
Here is a quick table:

129
00:08:47.811 --> 00:08:51.288
Authentication (authN), and Authorization (authZ).

130
00:08:51.568 --> 00:08:52.443
Question.

131
00:08:52.723 --> 00:08:54.472
Authentication (authN):

132
00:08:54.752 --> 00:08:55.627
Who are you?

133
00:08:55.907 --> 00:08:57.635
Authorization (authZ):

134
00:08:57.915 --> 00:08:58.896
What may you do?

135
00:08:59.176 --> 00:08:59.923
Proof.

136
00:09:00.203 --> 00:09:01.952
Authentication (authN):

137
00:09:02.232 --> 00:09:04.515
Password, OTP, passkey, certificate.

138
00:09:04.795 --> 00:09:06.523
Authorization (authZ):

139
00:09:06.803 --> 00:09:09.683
Roles, attributes, relationships, policy.

140
00:09:09.963 --> 00:09:10.624
Where.

141
00:09:10.904 --> 00:09:12.653
Authentication (authN):

142
00:09:12.933 --> 00:09:15.451
At the edge or IdP, once per session.

143
00:09:15.731 --> 00:09:17.459
Authorization (authZ):

144
00:09:17.739 --> 00:09:19.936
On every request and every object.

145
00:09:20.216 --> 00:09:21.069
Failure.

146
00:09:21.349 --> 00:09:23.099
Authentication (authN):

147
00:09:23.379 --> 00:09:24.531
Impersonation.

148
00:09:24.811 --> 00:09:26.539
Authorization (authZ):

149
00:09:26.819 --> 00:09:29.635
Privilege escalation, data exposure (IDOR).

150
00:09:30.985 --> 00:09:37.299
The commonest security bug is conflating them: authenticating a request and treating that as authorization.

151
00:09:37.579 --> 00:09:45.537
L3's IDOR control shows a handler that authenticates fine and leaks every customer's orders because it never checks the object.

152
00:09:46.887 --> 00:09:49.319
Sessions, tokens and JWTs.

153
00:09:49.599 --> 00:09:53.759
Opaque session tokens: a random ID; the server looks up state.

154
00:09:54.039 --> 00:09:57.473
Easy to revoke (delete the row), needs a lookup, and is stateful.

155
00:09:57.753 --> 00:10:03.428
JWTs (self-contained): signed claims the resource server validates without a lookup.

156
00:10:03.708 --> 00:10:11.644
Scales statelessly, but is hard to revoke before expiry, so keep them short-lived and pair them with a revocation list for logout.

157
00:10:11.924 --> 00:10:18.324
A JWT is base64url(header).base64url(claims).signature.

158
00:10:18.604 --> 00:10:19.863
Validation must:

159
00:10:20.143 --> 00:10:24.068
Fix the algorithm from your key config, never from the token's header.

160
00:10:24.348 --> 00:10:34.161
Accepting alg: none or letting the token pick RS256 versus HS256 is the alg-confusion vulnerability.

161
00:10:35.511 --> 00:10:47.991
Resolve the key by kid against a trusted JWKS, never a key embedded in the header (jwk or jku or x5u) or fetched from a token-supplied URL.

162
00:10:48.271 --> 00:10:49.722
Verify the signature.

163
00:10:50.002 --> 00:10:56.893
Check registered claims: iss, aud, exp, nbf (with small clock skew), and reject absurd lifetimes.

164
00:10:57.173 --> 00:11:02.122
Check revocation (jti against a denylist) for logout or compromise.

165
00:11:02.402 --> 00:11:06.626
L1's naive validator skips 1 to 2 and 4 to 5, and accepts:

