230
00:13:27.421 --> 00:13:28.594
4 µs.

231
00:13:28.874 --> 00:13:29.941
Token size:

232
00:13:30.221 --> 00:13:31.586
283 B.

233
00:13:31.866 --> 00:13:32.570
Use:

234
00:13:32.850 --> 00:13:36.733
Symmetric; only when signer and verifier are the same trust domain.

235
00:13:38.083 --> 00:13:48.152
Asymmetric (RS256 or ES256) lets resource servers verify with a public key without holding a signing secret.

236
00:13:48.432 --> 00:14:00.635
That's why the HS256 with the public key as the secret attack matters: if the verifier accepts HS256, an attacker signs with the well-known public key.

237
00:14:01.985 --> 00:14:03.478
OTP and passwords.

238
00:14:03.758 --> 00:14:06.019
The grocery app uses phone OTP.

239
00:14:06.299 --> 00:14:07.899
The controls (L2 A):

240
00:14:08.179 --> 00:14:14.579
Attempt limit per code (lock after ~5), so a 6-digit code (1,000,000 values) can't be guessed.

241
00:14:14.859 --> 00:14:16.758
Naive: unlimited; hardened:

242
00:14:17.038 --> 00:14:17.806
5.

243
00:14:18.086 --> 00:14:24.273
Send limit per phone (for example, 3 per 10 minutes) to stop SMS-bombing and cost abuse.

244
00:14:24.553 --> 00:14:29.310
Store codes hashed (salted) with a short expiry (5 minutes), single-use.

245
00:14:29.590 --> 00:14:31.681
Constant-time comparison of the hash.

246
00:14:31.961 --> 00:14:35.459
Don't leak whether the phone is registered (uniform responses).

247
00:14:36.809 --> 00:14:38.772
For passwords (staff, some flows):

248
00:14:39.052 --> 00:14:53.815
Argon2id (or scrypt or bcrypt), never fast hashes or encryption; per-user salt; a pepper in the KMS; check against breached-password lists; and rate-limit plus lockout with care not to enable denial of service.

249
00:14:54.095 --> 00:14:54.948
MFA:

250
00:14:55.228 --> 00:15:00.135
TOTP or, preferably, passkeys or WebAuthn (phishing-resistant, hardware-backed).

251
00:15:00.415 --> 00:15:05.919
SMS OTP is a usability floor, not strong MFA (SIM-swap risk).

252
00:15:07.269 --> 00:15:09.167
OAuth 2.0 and OIDC.

253
00:15:09.447 --> 00:15:17.085
OAuth 2.0 is authorization delegation (an app gets a token to call an API on the user's behalf).

254
00:15:17.365 --> 00:15:21.823
OIDC adds authentication on top (an idtoken proving who logged in).

255
00:15:22.103 --> 00:15:29.826
Roles: resource owner (the user), client (the app), authorization server (issues tokens), resource server (the API).

256
00:15:30.106 --> 00:15:30.981
Flows:

257
00:15:31.261 --> 00:15:38.045
Authorization Code + PKCE, for web and mobile apps, the only recommended interactive flow now.

258
00:15:38.325 --> 00:15:41.226
Client Credentials, service-to-service (no user).

259
00:15:42.576 --> 00:15:44.432
Device Code, TVs, CLIs.

260
00:15:44.712 --> 00:15:48.616
Implicit and Resource Owner Password, deprecated; don't use them.

261
00:15:48.896 --> 00:16:04.875
PKCE (RFC 7636): the app generates a random codeverifier, sends its SHA-256 hash (codechallenge) with the authorization request, and presents the verifier when exchanging the code.

262
00:16:05.155 --> 00:16:08.205
An intercepted code is useless without the verifier.

263
00:16:08.485 --> 00:16:10.981
S256 only, never plain.

264
00:16:11.261 --> 00:16:16.211
L2 B verifies the OAuth controls on a real HTTP server:

265
00:16:17.561 --> 00:16:18.926
Here is a quick table:

266
00:16:19.206 --> 00:16:24.582
Request presented to or token, and Naive server, and Hardened (Security BCP).

267
00:16:24.862 --> 00:16:27.294
Correct verifier, registered redirect.

268
00:16:27.574 --> 00:16:29.537
Naive server: token issued.

269
00:16:29.817 --> 00:16:32.718
Hardened (Security BCP): token issued.

270
00:16:32.998 --> 00:16:34.662
Wrong PKCE verifier.

271
00:16:34.942 --> 00:16:36.905
Naive server: token issued.

272
00:16:37.185 --> 00:16:40.555
Hardened (Security BCP): reject (PKCE).

273
00:16:40.835 --> 00:16:42.243
No verifier at all.

274
00:16:42.523 --> 00:16:44.486
Naive server: token issued.

275
00:16:44.766 --> 00:16:48.137
Hardened (Security BCP): reject (PKCE).

276
00:16:48.417 --> 00:16:50.209
Attacker-controlled redirecturi.

277
00:16:50.489 --> 00:16:52.451
Naive server: token issued.

278
00:16:52.731 --> 00:16:56.913
Hardened (Security BCP): reject (redirect not registered).

279
00:16:57.193 --> 00:16:59.411
Same code exchanged a second time.

280
00:16:59.691 --> 00:17:02.785
Hardened (Security BCP): reject (replay).

281
00:17:03.065 --> 00:17:04.942
First token after that replay.

282
00:17:05.222 --> 00:17:07.825
Hardened (Security BCP): revoked.

283
00:17:09.175 --> 00:17:26.839
The BCP rules L2 enforces: exact-match registered redirect URIs (no wildcards), PKCE for all clients, single-use codes (and on replay, revoke tokens already issued from that code), the state parameter for CSRF, and short-lived codes.

284
00:17:28.189 --> 00:17:30.215
Token lifecycle and rotation.

285
00:17:30.495 --> 00:17:33.546
Access tokens: short-lived (5 to 15 min).

286
00:17:33.826 --> 00:17:35.554
A leaked one expires quickly.

287
00:17:35.834 --> 00:17:38.479
Refresh tokens: longer-lived, rotating.

288
00:17:38.759 --> 00:17:42.599
Each use issues a new refresh token and invalidates the old one.

289
00:17:42.879 --> 00:17:53.461
If an old (already-used) refresh token appears again, that's reuse detection: someone has a stolen copy, so revoke the whole token family and force re-login.

290
00:17:53.741 --> 00:17:57.517
L1 C models a refresh token stolen from a phone backup:

291
00:17:57.797 --> 00:18:06.671
Long-lived reusable refresh token: the attacker kept access for 43,200 minutes (the full 30 days), unnoticed.

292
00:18:08.021 --> 00:18:20.949
Rotation + reuse detection: the attacker got 15 minutes (one access token); the customer's next refresh presented the now-used token, reuse was detected, and the family was revoked.

293
00:18:21.229 --> 00:18:32.899
Key rotation (JWKS): publish the new signing key alongside the old, sign with the new one, keep verifying both until older tokens expire, then retire the old key.

294
00:18:33.179 --> 00:18:39.387
L1 B: during the overlap both tokens validate; after retirement, the old kid is rejected.

295
00:18:39.667 --> 00:18:42.099
Never rotate by deleting the current key.

296
00:18:43.449 --> 00:18:45.774
Zero trust and service-to-service auth.

297
00:18:46.054 --> 00:18:49.553
Zero trust: no implicit trust from network location.

298
00:18:49.833 --> 00:18:55.251
Every request is authenticated and authorized, inside the perimeter as much as outside.

299
00:18:55.531 --> 00:19:03.787
Service identity: each workload gets a cryptographic identity (SPIFFE or SPIRE issues SVIDs), used for mTLS.

300
00:19:04.067 --> 00:19:10.894
The service mesh (Ch 17) can enforce mTLS and identity-based policy transparently.

301
00:19:11.174 --> 00:19:21.051
Short-lived credentials: certificates and tokens minted per workload, rotated automatically (minutes to hours), so a leaked credential is briefly useful.

302
00:19:22.401 --> 00:19:38.593
Authorization between services: the calling service's identity plus the end-user context (a propagated token or a signed request) decide access. payment-service may call ledger-service's POST or postings is policy, not firewall.

303
00:19:38.873 --> 00:19:52.356
Least privilege and segmentation: default-deny network policy, per-service DB users with minimal grants, and dynamic DB credentials from the KMS (short TTL) instead of a shared password.

304
00:19:53.706 --> 00:19:55.157
Authorization models:

305
00:19:55.437 --> 00:19:56.930
RBAC, ABAC, ReBAC.

306
00:19:57.210 --> 00:20:01.029
L3 runs the same 12 grocery requests through all three:

307
00:20:02.379 --> 00:20:03.744
Here is a quick table:

308
00:20:04.024 --> 00:20:06.840
Model, and Decides by, and Good at, and Weak at.

309
00:20:07.120 --> 00:20:07.973
RBAC.

310
00:20:08.253 --> 00:20:09.320
Decides by:

311
00:20:09.600 --> 00:20:11.605
The subject's roles, then permissions.

312
00:20:11.885 --> 00:20:12.675
Good at:

313
00:20:12.955 --> 00:20:15.557
Simple, auditable, coarse-grained org roles.

314
00:20:15.837 --> 00:20:16.691
Weak at:

315
00:20:16.971 --> 00:20:22.411
Can't express own, same store, at most limit, with purpose (role explosion if you try).

316
00:20:22.691 --> 00:20:23.480
ABAC.

317
00:20:23.760 --> 00:20:24.827
Decides by:

318
00:20:25.107 --> 00:20:29.395
Attributes of subject or resource or action or env, via policy.

319
00:20:29.675 --> 00:20:30.464
Good at:

320
00:20:30.744 --> 00:20:34.349
Fine-grained conditions, context (time, amount, purpose).

321
00:20:34.629 --> 00:20:35.483
Weak at:

322
00:20:35.763 --> 00:20:39.432
Policies get complex; hard to answer who can access X?

323
00:20:39.712 --> 00:20:40.501
ReBAC.

324
00:20:40.781 --> 00:20:41.848
Decides by:

325
00:20:42.128 --> 00:20:44.453
Relationships in a graph (Zanzibar).

326
00:20:44.733 --> 00:20:45.523
Good at:

327
00:20:45.803 --> 00:20:50.645
Manager of the store that owns this order, inherited or nested access, sharing.

328
00:20:50.925 --> 00:20:51.779
Weak at:

329
00:20:52.059 --> 00:20:55.579
Needs the graph and a check service; conditions still need ABAC.

330
00:20:56.929 --> 00:20:58.529
L3's result table:

331
00:20:58.809 --> 00:21:12.782
RBAC alone allowed: the IDOR read (customer, then another's order), the cross-store update, the 8,000 rupeesover-limit refund, the Bengaluru manager reading a Mumbai order, and the PII read with no ticket.

332
00:21:13.062 --> 00:21:15.089
Roles simply can't say no to these.

333
00:21:15.369 --> 00:21:23.902
ABAC denied all of them via ownership, store scope, a 5,000 rupeesrefund limit, city scope, and an open ticket purpose check.

334
00:21:25.252 --> 00:21:37.476
ReBAC answered order reads from the graph: owner, delivering rider, and manager via store, then city inheritance, without the order row carrying storeId or city; the edges carry it.

335
00:21:37.756 --> 00:21:39.761
In practice, platforms layer them:

336
00:21:40.041 --> 00:21:55.935
RBAC for coarse org roles, ABAC conditions for limits and context, and ReBAC (an authorization service like OpenFGA or SpiceDB, or a policy engine like OPA or Cedar) for object- and relationship-level checks.

337
00:21:56.215 --> 00:22:01.932
The decision is centralized (policy) but enforced locally (each service checks before acting).

338
00:22:03.282 --> 00:22:06.759
The OWASP API Security Top 10, on this platform.

339
00:22:07.039 --> 00:22:08.405
Here is a quick table:

340
00:22:08.685 --> 00:22:11.117
Risk, and Grocery example, and Defense.

341
00:22:11.397 --> 00:22:12.527
API1.

342
00:22:12.807 --> 00:22:13.511
Risk:

343
00:22:13.791 --> 00:22:16.543
Broken Object Level Authorization (IDOR).

344
00:22:16.823 --> 00:22:20.727
Grocery example: /orders/id returns any order.

345
00:22:21.007 --> 00:22:21.882
Defense:

346
00:22:22.162 --> 00:22:24.807
Object-level check on every fetch (L3).

347
00:22:25.087 --> 00:22:26.239
API2.

348
00:22:26.519 --> 00:22:27.223
Risk:

349
00:22:27.503 --> 00:22:28.975
Broken Authentication.

350
00:22:29.255 --> 00:22:32.669
Grocery example: alg:none, no OTP limit.

351
00:22:32.949 --> 00:22:33.823
Defense:

352
00:22:34.103 --> 00:22:37.389
Hardened JWT + OTP (L1, L2).

353
00:22:37.669 --> 00:22:38.842
API3.

354
00:22:39.122 --> 00:22:39.826
Risk:

355
00:22:40.106 --> 00:22:42.133
Broken Object Property Level Auth.

356
00:22:42.413 --> 00:22:43.650
Grocery example:

357
00:22:43.930 --> 00:22:47.087
Client sets role or price in the body (mass assignment).

358
00:22:47.367 --> 00:22:48.242
Defense:

359
00:22:48.522 --> 00:22:52.661
Allow-list writable fields; never trust client-set authority fields.

360
00:22:52.941 --> 00:22:54.071
API4.

361
00:22:54.351 --> 00:22:55.055
Risk:

362
00:22:55.335 --> 00:22:57.319
Unrestricted Resource Consumption.

363
00:22:57.599 --> 00:22:58.837
Grocery example:

364
00:22:59.117 --> 00:23:01.506
Unbounded page size, no rate limit.

365
00:23:01.786 --> 00:23:02.661
Defense:

366
00:23:02.941 --> 00:23:06.077
Limits, quotas, pagination caps (Ch 18).

367
00:23:06.357 --> 00:23:07.509
API5.

368
00:23:07.789 --> 00:23:08.493
Risk:

369
00:23:08.773 --> 00:23:10.991
Broken Function Level Authorization.

370
00:23:11.271 --> 00:23:12.509
Grocery example:

371
00:23:12.789 --> 00:23:14.751
Customer calls an admin endpoint.

372
00:23:15.031 --> 00:23:15.906
Defense:

373
00:23:16.186 --> 00:23:18.746
Deny by default; check function permission.

374
00:23:20.096 --> 00:23:21.312
API6.

375
00:23:21.592 --> 00:23:22.296
Risk:

376
00:23:22.576 --> 00:23:25.520
Unrestricted Access to Sensitive Business Flows.

377
00:23:25.800 --> 00:23:27.037
Grocery example:

378
00:23:27.317 --> 00:23:29.216
Bots draining flash-sale stock.

379
00:23:29.496 --> 00:23:30.371
Defense:

380
00:23:30.651 --> 00:23:32.912
Bot defense, per-user flow limits.

381
00:23:33.192 --> 00:23:34.387
API7.

382
00:23:34.667 --> 00:23:35.371
Risk:

383
00:23:35.651 --> 00:23:36.696
SSRF.

384
00:23:36.976 --> 00:23:42.032
Grocery example: image URL fetched by the server hits internal metadata.

385
00:23:42.312 --> 00:23:43.187
Defense:

386
00:23:43.467 --> 00:23:47.413
Allow-list egress, no raw URLs (Ch 21 lesson).

387
00:23:47.693 --> 00:23:48.845
API8.

388
00:23:49.125 --> 00:23:49.829
Risk:

389
00:23:50.109 --> 00:23:52.072
Security Misconfiguration.

390
00:23:52.352 --> 00:23:53.589
Grocery example:

391
00:23:53.869 --> 00:23:56.024
Debug endpoints, permissive CORS.

392
00:23:56.304 --> 00:23:57.179
Defense:

393
00:23:57.459 --> 00:23:59.656
Hardened baselines, config review.

394
00:23:59.936 --> 00:24:01.088
API9.

395
00:24:01.368 --> 00:24:02.072
Risk:

396
00:24:02.352 --> 00:24:04.165
Improper Inventory Management.

397
00:24:04.445 --> 00:24:05.683
Grocery example:

398
00:24:05.963 --> 00:24:07.925
Forgotten or v1 with no auth.

399
00:24:08.205 --> 00:24:09.080
Defense:

400
00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:12.048
API catalog, retire old versions.

401
00:24:12.328 --> 00:24:13.459
API10.

402
00:24:13.739 --> 00:24:14.443
Risk:

403
00:24:14.723 --> 00:24:17.475
Unsafe Consumption of 3rd-party APIs.

404
00:24:17.755 --> 00:24:18.992
Grocery example:

405
00:24:19.272 --> 00:24:21.277
Trusting a gateway callback blindly.

406
00:24:21.557 --> 00:24:22.432
Defense:

407
00:24:22.712 --> 00:24:26.104
Verify signatures or idempotency (Ch 20).

408
00:24:27.454 --> 00:24:38.227
Injection (SQLi, command injection) sits under secure coding: parameterized queries always (Ch 05), never string-built SQL or shell.

409
00:24:39.577 --> 00:24:40.943
Secrets management.

410
00:24:41.223 --> 00:24:45.084
Never in code or Git, never in plain env vars or images.

411
00:24:45.364 --> 00:24:50.484
Use a secrets manager (Vault or OpenBao, cloud KMS or Secrets Manager).

412
00:24:50.764 --> 00:25:02.199
Dynamic secrets: the KMS mints short-lived DB credentials per service on demand and revokes them on TTL, so there's no shared, long-lived DB password to leak.

413
00:25:02.479 --> 00:25:06.191
Rotation: automated, with overlap (like JWKS).

414
00:25:06.471 --> 00:25:12.316
Access: authenticated by workload identity, least-privilege policies, and every read audited.

415
00:25:13.666 --> 00:25:20.621
Detection: secret-scanning in CI and on commits; treat any committed secret as compromised and rotate it.

416
00:25:21.971 --> 00:25:22.845
Encryption.

417
00:25:23.125 --> 00:25:24.064
In transit:

418
00:25:24.344 --> 00:25:31.448
TLS 1.3, HSTS, modern ciphers; mTLS internally (§2.7).

419
00:25:31.728 --> 00:25:34.267
Terminate at the edge, re-encrypt internally.

420
00:25:34.547 --> 00:25:40.477
At rest: full-disk or volume encryption is table stakes but only protects against stolen disks.

421
00:25:40.757 --> 00:25:48.651
Application-level encryption protects specific fields (PII, payment data) against a compromised database or backup.

422
00:25:48.931 --> 00:25:52.003
Envelope encryption (L4) is the standard pattern:

423
00:25:52.283 --> 00:25:57.125
Generate a data encryption key (DEK) per record (or per tenant or batch).

424
00:25:58.475 --> 00:26:09.782
Encrypt the field with the DEK using authenticated encryption (AES-256-GCM), a unique IV per encryption, and a tag that detects tampering.

425
00:26:10.062 --> 00:26:16.633
Wrap (encrypt) the DEK with a key-encryption key (KEK) that lives in the KMS and never leaves it.

426
00:26:16.913 --> 00:26:20.795
Store ciphertext + wrapped DEK + KEK version.

427
00:26:21.075 --> 00:26:24.019
To read, ask the KMS to unwrap the DEK.

428
00:26:24.299 --> 00:26:26.625
L4 with OpenBao's transit engine:

429
00:26:27.975 --> 00:26:39.345
A 60-byte address, then 76-byte ciphertext + a 12-byte IV + an 89-byte wrapped DEK; the row holds no plaintext and no key material.

430
00:26:39.625 --> 00:26:49.076
Tampering: flipping one ciphertext bit makes GCM decryption throw AEADBadTagException, the record is rejected, not silently corrupted.

431
00:26:49.356 --> 00:27:02.625
KEK rotation: rotate the KEK in the KMS; old rows still decrypt (the KMS keeps old versions), and you rewrap only the small DEKs (kilobytes), never the data (terabytes).

432
00:27:02.905 --> 00:27:05.401
The ciphertext is byte-for-byte unchanged.

433
00:27:06.751 --> 00:27:22.282
Cost: a KMS call per record was 1.72 ms or field; reusing one wrapped DEK across records (each with its own IV) was 0.015 ms or field, 115x cheaper.

434
00:27:22.562 --> 00:27:27.063
Cache or derive DEKs per tenant or batch; never cache the KEK.

435
00:27:28.413 --> 00:27:30.717
Crypto-shredding and the right to erasure.

436
00:27:30.997 --> 00:27:40.491
Delete all my data (DPDP or GDPR, Ch 36) is hard because data spreads into backups, replicas, logs and the lake.

437
00:27:40.771 --> 00:27:46.659
Crypto-shredding: encrypt a customer's data under a per-customer key; to erase, destroy the key.

438
00:27:46.939 --> 00:27:51.227
Every copy, everywhere, becomes permanently unrecoverable ciphertext.

439
00:27:51.507 --> 00:28:03.005
L4 D: after destroying the customer's KEK in the KMS, unwrapping the DEK fails, so the data is gone everywhere it was copied, without hunting down every backup.

440
00:28:03.285 --> 00:28:16.832
Caveats: it only works if the customer's data was only ever encrypted under that key (no plaintext copies escaped), and you still delete what you can directly; shredding covers the long tail (backups, archives).

441
00:28:18.182 --> 00:28:20.166
Defense in depth and threat modeling.

442
00:28:20.446 --> 00:28:22.302
No single control is trusted.

443
00:28:22.582 --> 00:28:37.238
Layers: edge (WAF, TLS, rate limits), gateway (authN, coarse authZ), service (fine authZ, input validation), data (encryption, least-privilege access), and monitoring (audit logs, anomaly detection).

444
00:28:37.518 --> 00:28:39.651
Threat modeling (STRIDE) per feature:

445
00:28:39.931 --> 00:28:57.766
Spoofing, then strong authN; Tampering, then signatures, GCM, integrity; Repudiation, then audit logs; Information disclosure, then encryption, authZ; Denial of service, then rate limits (Ch 18); Elevation of privilege, then least privilege, authZ checks.

446
00:28:58.046 --> 00:29:04.531
Do it at design time, keep a risk register, and re-test with the conformance harnesses these labs illustrate.

447
00:29:05.881 --> 00:29:06.777
Part 3.

448
00:29:07.057 --> 00:29:08.785
Architecture and Diagrams.

449
00:29:10.035 --> 00:29:11.102
Component view.

450
00:29:11.382 --> 00:29:12.726
Here is the diagram.

451
00:29:16.576 --> 00:29:21.056
Sequence: happy path, mobile login with OAuth Code + PKCE.

452
00:29:21.336 --> 00:29:24.003
The sequence diagram walks through it step by step.

453
00:29:24.283 --> 00:29:30.427
App to App: generate codeverifier, codechallenge = S256(verifier).

454
00:29:30.707 --> 00:29:31.645
App to AS:

455
00:29:31.925 --> 00:29:36.277
OTP login, then or authorize with challenge, registered redirect.

456
00:29:36.557 --> 00:29:39.075
AS to App: redirect with one-time code.

457
00:29:39.355 --> 00:29:42.533
App to AS: /token with code + codeverifier.

458
00:29:42.813 --> 00:29:50.088
AS to AS: verify code unused, redirect matches, S256(verifier) = challenge.

459
00:29:50.368 --> 00:29:54.656
AS to App: short-lived access token + rotating refresh token.

460
00:29:54.936 --> 00:29:57.773
App to API: request with access token.

461
00:29:58.053 --> 00:30:07.120
API to API: validate JWT (fixed alg, JWKS kid, iss or aud or exp), authZ on the object.

462
00:30:07.400 --> 00:30:09.128
API to App: data.

463
00:30:12.978 --> 00:30:16.498
Sequence: failure path, refresh-token reuse detected.

464
00:30:16.778 --> 00:30:19.445
The sequence diagram walks through it step by step.

465
00:30:19.725 --> 00:30:23.053
Attacker to AS: refresh with stolen token RT1.

466
00:30:23.333 --> 00:30:28.517
AS to Attacker: new access token + RT2, RT1 marked used.

467
00:30:28.797 --> 00:30:33.127
Owner to AS: refresh with RT1 (the copy the owner still has).

468
00:30:33.407 --> 00:30:34.303
AS to AS:

469
00:30:34.583 --> 00:30:37.549
RT1 already used, then reuse detected.

470
00:30:37.829 --> 00:30:41.733
AS to Owner: revoke the whole token family, force re-login.

471
00:30:45.583 --> 00:30:50.084
State: envelope-encrypted record through KEK rotation and shredding.

472
00:30:50.364 --> 00:30:51.708
Here is the diagram.

