00:39:46.158 --> 00:39:51.833
L1, JWT validation, key rotation and refresh-token reuse detection.

624
00:39:52.113 --> 00:39:53.051
Requirement:

625
00:39:53.331 --> 00:39:56.873
A hand-rolled validator accepted a forged admin token.

626
00:39:57.153 --> 00:39:59.030
Show what a safe validator checks.

627
00:39:59.310 --> 00:40:21.753
The program builds JWTs with the JDK's own crypto (RS256 or ES256 or HS256), then runs a battery of good and forged tokens through a naive validator (trusts the header) and a hardened one (fixed algorithm per kid, JWKS, all registered claims, denylist).

628
00:40:22.033 --> 00:40:27.601
It also rotates keys through a JWKS and models refresh-token rotation.

629
00:40:28.951 --> 00:40:31.767
Here is the Java code, TokensL1.java.

630
00:40:32.047 --> 00:40:36.377
It runs to 291 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

631
00:40:36.657 --> 00:40:44.295
TokensL1.java, run with: java TokensL1.java (JDK 17 plus, no dependencies).

632
00:40:44.575 --> 00:41:06.569
JWTs built from the JDK's own crypto: issue RS256 or ES256 or HS256 tokens, then validate a battery of good and forged tokens. with a NAIVE validator (trusts the header) and a HARDENED one (allow-listed alg per kid, iss or aud or exp or nbf, denylist).

633
00:41:09.419 --> 00:41:10.337
On screen:

634
00:41:10.617 --> 00:41:11.235
A.

635
00:41:11.515 --> 00:41:26.747
Validating tokens presented to payment-api. token naive validator hardened validator. valid token (payment API audience) ACCEPTED ACCEPTED (valid). alg:none, no signature ACCEPTED rejected (unsigned).

636
00:41:27.027 --> 00:41:48.851
HS256 signed with our PUBLIC key as secret ACCEPTED rejected (alg mismatch). attacker key embedded in header (jwk) ACCEPTED rejected (key in header). payload edited (role, then admin), old signature rejected rejected (bad signature). expired 2 hours ago ACCEPTED rejected (expired). valid,

637
00:41:49.131 --> 00:42:02.571
but audience rider-app ACCEPTED rejected (aud). valid, but jti revoked at logout ACCEPTED rejected (revoked). valid signature, 30-day lifetime ACCEPTED rejected (lifetime too long).

638
00:42:02.851 --> 00:42:03.534
B.

639
00:42:03.814 --> 00:42:15.014
Key rotation through the JWKS (kid k1, then k2). overlap (JWKS has k1 plusk2): old-key token valid, new-key token valid.

640
00:42:15.294 --> 00:42:16.809
The rest is in the chapter.

641
00:42:19.659 --> 00:42:20.512
Reading it:

642
00:42:20.792 --> 00:42:24.397
The naive validator accepts 7 of 9 bad tokens.

643
00:42:24.677 --> 00:42:43.941
Trusting alg from the header gives alg:none and the HS256-with-public-key confusion; trusting an embedded jwk lets the token bring its own key; skipping exp or aud or jti or lifetime lets expired, wrong-audience, revoked and absurdly long-lived tokens through.

644
00:42:44.221 --> 00:42:48.147
Only actual signature tampering fails, because that breaks the maths.

645
00:42:49.497 --> 00:43:06.670
The hardened validator fixes the algorithm from the trusted key (never the token), resolves the key by kid in the JWKS, refuses keys in the header, and checks issuer, audience, expiry, not-before (with 30 s skew), a sane lifetime and revocation.

646
00:43:06.950 --> 00:43:18.193
Rotation: publish the new key first, so both validate during the overlap; retire the old kid only after the maximum token lifetime, so no in-flight token is orphaned.

647
00:43:19.543 --> 00:43:26.988
Refresh reuse: a stolen, long-lived reusable token gave 43,200 minutes of access.

648
00:43:27.268 --> 00:43:37.700
With rotation and reuse detection, the attacker got one 15-minute access token before the owner's next refresh exposed the reuse and revoked the family.

649
00:43:37.980 --> 00:43:38.961
Algorithms:

650
00:43:39.241 --> 00:43:54.409
RS256 verifies fast (47 µs) but signs slowly; ES256 has smaller tokens; HS256 is fastest but symmetric (only within one trust boundary).

