698
00:48:49.709 --> 00:49:10.979
n or a. store manager refunds 1,499 rupeesin their store n or a. store manager refunds 8,000 rupees (over limit), n or a. city manager reads a Bengaluru store order. city manager reads a Mumbai order. support reads customer PII (open ticket) n or a.

699
00:49:11.259 --> 00:49:12.773
The rest is in the chapter.

700
00:49:15.623 --> 00:49:16.477
Reading it:

701
00:49:16.757 --> 00:49:27.935
RBAC allows everything the role can ever do, so it can't deny the IDOR read, the cross-store update, the over-limit refund, the out-of-city read, or the no-purpose PII read.

702
00:49:28.215 --> 00:49:36.023
Trying to encode those as roles causes role explosion (storemanagerBLR17refundupto5000).

703
00:49:36.303 --> 00:49:52.389
ABAC expresses them directly: ownership (subject.id == resource.owner), store scope, a 5,000 rupeesrefund limit, city scope, and an open ticket purpose condition that flips with the environment (the no ticket row denies).

704
00:49:53.739 --> 00:50:03.445
ReBAC answers order reads from the relationship graph: owner, delivering rider, and manager reached through order, then parentstore, then parentcity.

705
00:50:03.725 --> 00:50:08.845
The order row doesn't need storeId or city columns, the edges carry the structure.

706
00:50:09.125 --> 00:50:14.736
Amount limits still need an ABAC-style condition, which is why real systems layer the models.

707
00:50:15.016 --> 00:50:25.192
IDOR control: the vulnerable handler leaks every order to any authenticated customer; adding the object-level ownership check denies the ones that aren't theirs.

708
00:50:25.472 --> 00:50:30.315
Authentication is not authorization, and the check must be against that object.

709
00:50:31.665 --> 00:50:36.891
L4, Envelope encryption, rotation and crypto-shredding with a real KMS.

710
00:50:37.171 --> 00:50:38.110
Requirement:

711
00:50:38.390 --> 00:50:47.393
Encrypt PII so a stolen database is useless, rotate keys without re-encrypting terabytes, and make 'delete my data' actually stick.

712
00:50:47.673 --> 00:50:58.979
The program uses OpenBao's transit engine as a real KMS (the KEK never leaves it) and the JDK's AES-256-GCM for the data.

713
00:50:59.259 --> 00:51:10.843
It seals a field, detects tampering, rotates the KEK and rewraps, crypto-shreds a customer key, and compares the cost of a KMS call per record against a cached DEK.

714
00:51:12.193 --> 00:51:15.052
Here is the Java code, CryptoL4.java.

715
00:51:15.332 --> 00:51:19.705
It runs to 164 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

716
00:51:19.985 --> 00:51:33.297
CryptoL4.java, run with: java CryptoL4.java (JDK 17 plus; needs a Vault or OpenBao transit engine at $BAO, default:8210).

717
00:51:33.577 --> 00:51:36.863
Encryption at rest done properly: envelope encryption.

718
00:51:37.143 --> 00:51:50.711
A per-record data key (DEK) encrypts the field with AES-256-GCM;. the DEK is wrapped by a key-encryption key (KEK) that never leaves the KMS (OpenBao transit).

719
00:51:50.991 --> 00:51:52.911
We store only ciphertext +.

720
00:51:55.761 --> 00:51:56.678
On screen:

721
00:51:56.958 --> 00:51:57.577
A.

722
00:51:57.857 --> 00:52:17.398
Envelope encryption of a customer's address (AES-256-GCM per record, DEK wrapped by. the KMS KEK). plaintext (60 bytes), then ciphertext 76 bytes, iv 12 bytes, wrapped DEK 89 bytes, KEK. version 2. what the database row stores:

723
00:52:17.678 --> 00:52:24.995
ACZvPdn9qYOVSgAGu or RqTLnLwRZxOZim...

724
00:52:25.275 --> 00:52:30.289
(no plaintext, no. key material). decrypt round-trip matches original: true.

725
00:52:30.569 --> 00:52:31.251
B.

726
00:52:31.531 --> 00:52:41.643
Authenticated encryption detects tampering (GCM tag). one flipped bit, then AEADBadTagException (record rejected, not silently corrupted).

727
00:52:41.923 --> 00:52:42.606
C.

728
00:52:42.886 --> 00:52:46.001
KEK rotation without re-encrypting the data.

729
00:52:46.281 --> 00:52:49.331
KEK rotated to version 3 in the KMS.

730
00:52:49.611 --> 00:52:56.331
Existing rows keep working (KMS keeps old. versions to unwrap): old row still decrypts: true.

731
00:52:56.611 --> 00:52:58.126
The rest is in the chapter.

732
00:53:00.976 --> 00:53:01.829
Reading it:

733
00:53:02.109 --> 00:53:10.835
Envelope: the row stores ciphertext + IV + wrapped DEK + KEK version, and no plaintext or key material.

734
00:53:11.115 --> 00:53:14.059
Reading asks the KMS to unwrap the DEK.

735
00:53:14.339 --> 00:53:20.483
Tamper detection: one flipped ciphertext bit makes GCM throw AEADBadTagException.

736
00:53:20.763 --> 00:53:26.843
Authenticated encryption means corrupted or altered data is rejected, not silently returned.

737
00:53:28.193 --> 00:53:35.147
Rotation: rotating the KEK in the KMS leaves old rows readable (the KMS keeps old versions).

738
00:53:35.427 --> 00:53:40.825
Rewrapping touches only the small DEK; the ciphertext is byte-for-byte identical.

739
00:53:41.105 --> 00:53:46.395
So rotating the master key rewraps kilobytes of DEKs, never terabytes of data.

740
00:53:46.675 --> 00:54:02.910
Crypto-shredding: with PII under a per-customer KEK, destroying that key makes every copy of the DEK, and therefore all that customer's ciphertext, in backups and replicas included, permanently unrecoverable, without hunting down every copy.

741
00:54:04.260 --> 00:54:18.767
Cost: a KMS round-trip per record was 1.72 ms; reusing one wrapped DEK across records (each with a fresh IV) was 0.015 ms, 115x cheaper.

742
00:54:19.047 --> 00:54:25.767
Cache or derive DEKs per tenant or batch; never cache the KEK; always use a unique IV.

743
00:54:27.117 --> 00:54:27.757
