750
00:56:13.187 --> 00:56:14.126
Part 6.

751
00:56:14.406 --> 00:56:15.686
Production Concerns.

752
00:56:16.936 --> 00:56:18.088
Failure modes.

753
00:56:18.368 --> 00:56:19.733
Here is a quick table:

754
00:56:20.013 --> 00:56:22.936
Failure, and Symptom, and Cause, and Mitigation.

755
00:56:23.216 --> 00:56:24.880
Token forgery accepted.

756
00:56:25.160 --> 00:56:25.971
Symptom:

757
00:56:26.251 --> 00:56:29.365
Privilege escalation, admin actions by users.

758
00:56:29.645 --> 00:56:30.413
Cause:

759
00:56:30.693 --> 00:56:32.848
Header-trusting validation (L1).

760
00:56:33.128 --> 00:56:34.131
Mitigation:

761
00:56:34.411 --> 00:56:38.507
Library validation, fixed alg, JWKS, claim checks.

762
00:56:38.787 --> 00:56:39.981
Account takeover.

763
00:56:40.261 --> 00:56:41.072
Symptom:

764
00:56:41.352 --> 00:56:43.613
Logins from new devices, complaints.

765
00:56:43.893 --> 00:56:44.661
Cause:

766
00:56:44.941 --> 00:56:49.037
No OTP attempt or send limits (L2), password reuse.

767
00:56:49.317 --> 00:56:50.320
Mitigation:

768
00:56:50.600 --> 00:56:54.717
Lockout, send caps, passkeys or MFA, breached-password checks.

769
00:56:54.997 --> 00:56:56.661
IDOR or data exposure.

770
00:56:56.941 --> 00:56:57.752
Symptom:

771
00:56:58.032 --> 00:57:00.080
One customer sees another's data.

772
00:57:00.360 --> 00:57:01.128
Cause:

773
00:57:01.408 --> 00:57:04.693
Role-only check, no object-level authZ (L3).

774
00:57:04.973 --> 00:57:05.976
Mitigation:

775
00:57:06.256 --> 00:57:09.691
Object-level checks on every fetch; deny by default.

776
00:57:09.971 --> 00:57:11.656
Privilege via role gaps.

777
00:57:11.936 --> 00:57:12.747
Symptom:

778
00:57:13.027 --> 00:57:15.395
Over-limit refunds, cross-store edits.

779
00:57:15.675 --> 00:57:16.443
Cause:

780
00:57:16.723 --> 00:57:18.301
Coarse RBAC (L3).

781
00:57:18.581 --> 00:57:19.584
Mitigation:

782
00:57:19.864 --> 00:57:22.723
ABAC conditions, ReBAC relationships.

783
00:57:24.073 --> 00:57:25.609
Signing key compromise.

784
00:57:25.889 --> 00:57:26.699
Symptom:

785
00:57:26.979 --> 00:57:28.302
Widespread forgery.

786
00:57:28.582 --> 00:57:29.350
Cause:

787
00:57:29.630 --> 00:57:30.889
Leaked private key.

788
00:57:31.169 --> 00:57:32.171
Mitigation:

789
00:57:32.451 --> 00:57:38.979
JWKS rotation, short tokens, family revocation, HSM or KMS-held keys.

790
00:57:39.259 --> 00:57:40.646
Refresh token theft.

791
00:57:40.926 --> 00:57:41.737
Symptom:

792
00:57:42.017 --> 00:57:43.233
Persistent access.

793
00:57:43.513 --> 00:57:44.281
Cause:

794
00:57:44.561 --> 00:57:47.867
Long-lived, reusable refresh tokens (L1 C).

795
00:57:48.147 --> 00:57:49.150
Mitigation:

796
00:57:49.430 --> 00:57:51.499
Rotation + reuse detection.

797
00:57:51.779 --> 00:57:52.803
Secret leak.

798
00:57:53.083 --> 00:57:53.894
Symptom:

799
00:57:54.174 --> 00:57:55.817
Creds in a repo or image.

800
00:57:56.097 --> 00:57:56.865
Cause:

801
00:57:57.145 --> 00:57:58.787
Secrets in code or env.

802
00:57:59.067 --> 00:58:00.070
Mitigation:

803
00:58:00.350 --> 00:58:04.531
KMS, dynamic secrets, secret scanning, rotate on exposure.

804
00:58:04.811 --> 00:58:06.689
Data breach reads plaintext.

805
00:58:06.969 --> 00:58:07.779
Symptom:

806
00:58:08.059 --> 00:58:11.046
PII exposed from a DB or backup dump.

807
00:58:11.326 --> 00:58:12.094
Cause:

808
00:58:12.374 --> 00:58:13.718
No field encryption.

809
00:58:13.998 --> 00:58:15.001
Mitigation:

810
00:58:15.281 --> 00:58:18.374
Envelope encryption; disk encryption is not enough.

811
00:58:18.654 --> 00:58:19.891
Cannot erase data.

812
00:58:20.171 --> 00:58:20.982
Symptom:

813
00:58:21.262 --> 00:58:22.606
Regulatory failure.

814
00:58:22.886 --> 00:58:23.654
Cause:

815
00:58:23.934 --> 00:58:26.345
PII copied everywhere in plaintext.

816
00:58:26.625 --> 00:58:27.627
Mitigation:

817
00:58:27.907 --> 00:58:29.742
Crypto-shredding with per-tenant keys.

818
00:58:31.092 --> 00:58:32.287
Lateral movement.

819
00:58:32.567 --> 00:58:33.377
Symptom:

820
00:58:33.657 --> 00:58:35.705
One popped service reaches everything.

821
00:58:35.985 --> 00:58:36.753
Cause:

822
00:58:37.033 --> 00:58:38.868
Flat trust inside the perimeter.

823
00:58:39.148 --> 00:58:40.151
Mitigation:

824
00:58:40.431 --> 00:58:44.079
Zero trust, mTLS, segmentation, least privilege.

825
00:58:45.429 --> 00:58:47.050
Observability and audit.

826
00:58:47.330 --> 00:59:08.023
Security signals: authN failures by reason, token-validation failures by cause (a spike in alg mismatch/bad signature is probing), OTP lockouts, authZ denials by policy and object type, PII-access reads, KMS operations, mTLS handshake failures, WAF blocks.

827
00:59:08.303 --> 00:59:21.743
Audit log: immutable, tamper-evident (append-only, hash-chained or shipped to write-once storage), recording who did what to which object when, for sensitive actions (refunds, PII access, role changes).

828
00:59:22.023 --> 00:59:25.607
Required for forensics and compliance (Ch 36).

829
00:59:26.957 --> 00:59:35.576
Alerting: impossible-travel logins, sudden privilege use, bulk PII reads, a service suddenly calling endpoints it never called.

830
00:59:35.856 --> 00:59:36.923
Tie into SIEM.

831
00:59:37.203 --> 00:59:40.061
Don't log secrets or full tokens or PII.

832
00:59:40.341 --> 00:59:43.499
Redact; log a token's jti, not the token.

833
00:59:44.849 --> 00:59:47.665
Security (hardening the security system itself).

834
00:59:47.945 --> 00:59:52.169
The authorization service and KMS are now critical dependencies:

835
00:59:52.449 --> 00:59:59.190
HA, rate-limited, cached with fail-closed for sensitive actions, and themselves access-controlled and audited.

836
00:59:59.470 --> 01:00:12.078
Cache carefully: cached JWKS keys and cached authZ decisions need short TTLs so revocation and rotation take effect; too long a TTL is its own vulnerability.

837
01:00:12.358 --> 01:00:22.918
Protect the conformance tests and policies as code: policy changes go through review (a bad policy is a breach), and the CI harness must run on every change.

838
01:00:24.268 --> 01:00:25.932
Performance and tuning knobs.

839
01:00:26.212 --> 01:00:27.577
Here is a quick table:

840
01:00:27.857 --> 01:00:29.073
Knob, and Guidance.

841
01:00:29.353 --> 01:00:30.633
Token algorithm:

842
01:00:30.913 --> 01:00:38.487
RS256 default; ES256 for smaller tokens or cheaper signing (L1 D).

843
01:00:38.767 --> 01:00:40.345
Access token TTL:

844
01:00:40.625 --> 01:00:45.383
5 to 15 min; shorter = less revocation lag, more refresh load.

845
01:00:45.663 --> 01:00:47.071
JWKS cache:

846
01:00:47.351 --> 01:00:49.249
Minutes; refresh on unknown kid.

847
01:00:49.529 --> 01:00:50.745
AuthZ decisions:

848
01:00:51.025 --> 01:00:56.529
Cache per (subject, action, object) with a short TTL; batch checks for list endpoints.

849
01:00:56.809 --> 01:00:58.153
DEK strategy:

850
01:00:58.433 --> 01:01:02.359
Per-tenant or batch cached DEKs, per-record IVs (L4:

851
01:01:02.639 --> 01:01:05.796
115x versus per-record KMS).

852
01:01:06.076 --> 01:01:07.249
KMS calls:

853
01:01:07.529 --> 01:01:12.863
Batch; use the KMS for wrap or unwrap and data-key generation, not bulk data.

854
01:01:13.143 --> 01:01:14.401
Argon2id:

855
01:01:14.681 --> 01:01:22.255
Tune memory or iterations to ~100 to 250 ms per hash on your hardware. mTLS:

856
01:01:22.535 --> 01:01:26.012
Session resumption; rotate SVIDs on the order of hours.

857
01:01:27.362 --> 01:01:28.087
Cost.

858
01:01:28.367 --> 01:01:30.245
Security is cheaper than a breach:

859
01:01:30.525 --> 01:01:35.837
DPDP penalties, incident response, and lost trust dwarf the engineering cost.

860
01:01:36.117 --> 01:01:48.639
But controls have real cost, KMS calls, extra hops for authZ, MFA friction, so apply them proportionally to data sensitivity (a public product image needs none of L4).

861
01:01:48.919 --> 01:01:57.645
The expensive mistakes are architectural: retrofitting object-level authZ or field encryption into a system that assumed neither.

862
01:01:57.925 --> 01:02:01.039
Build them in from the start; that's why this is a chapter.

863
01:02:02.389 --> 01:02:03.328
Part 7.

864
01:02:03.608 --> 01:02:05.315
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

865
01:02:06.565 --> 01:02:07.205
1.

866
01:02:07.485 --> 01:02:14.375
Trusting the JWT header's algorithm (L1: alg:none and HS or RS confusion accepted).

867
01:02:15.725 --> 01:02:23.640
Here is the Java code. after: the algorithm and key come from YOUR config, keyed by kid in a trusted JWKS.

868
01:02:26.490 --> 01:02:27.194
2.

869
01:02:27.474 --> 01:02:29.885
Hand-rolling JWT or crypto.

870
01:02:30.165 --> 01:02:35.541
Use vetted libraries (Nimbus, jose4j; Tink or libsodium for encryption).

871
01:02:35.821 --> 01:02:38.743
L1 and L4 write it out to teach, not to ship.

872
01:02:39.023 --> 01:02:39.770
3.

873
01:02:40.050 --> 01:02:45.362
No brute-force protection on OTP or passwords (L2: unlimited guesses).

874
01:02:45.642 --> 01:02:49.909
Lockout, send caps, hashed+expiring codes, and prefer passkeys.

875
01:02:50.189 --> 01:02:50.893
4.

876
01:02:51.173 --> 01:02:55.183
Authentication mistaken for authorization or IDOR (L3).

877
01:02:56.533 --> 01:03:03.296
Here is the Java code. before: logged in ⇒ allowed. after: check the caller's relation to THIS object.

878
01:03:06.146 --> 01:03:06.914
5.

879
01:03:07.194 --> 01:03:09.391
Mass assignment of authority fields.

880
01:03:10.741 --> 01:03:22.304
Here is the Java code. before: binds the whole body, including role or price the client set. after: allow-list what the client may set; authority fields are server-controlled.

881
01:03:25.154 --> 01:03:25.901
6.

882
01:03:26.181 --> 01:03:31.109
RBAC for everything (L3: can't express own or scope or limit or purpose).

883
01:03:31.389 --> 01:03:34.759
Layer ABAC conditions and ReBAC relationships.

884
01:03:35.039 --> 01:03:35.786
7.

885
01:03:36.066 --> 01:03:38.903
Long-lived, reusable tokens (L1 C:

886
01:03:39.183 --> 01:03:40.634
30 days of access).

887
01:03:40.914 --> 01:03:45.159
Short access tokens, rotating refresh tokens with reuse detection.

888
01:03:45.439 --> 01:03:46.122
8.

889
01:03:46.402 --> 01:03:48.749
Secrets in code, env or images.

890
01:03:50.099 --> 01:03:56.093
Here is the YAML code. after: fetched at runtime from the KMS, short-lived and rotated.

891
01:03:58.943 --> 01:03:59.690
9.

892
01:03:59.970 --> 01:04:02.039
Only disk encryption for PII.

893
01:04:02.319 --> 01:04:06.735
It protects against stolen disks, not a compromised app or a leaked backup.

894
01:04:07.015 --> 01:04:10.450
Encrypt sensitive fields at the application layer (L4).

895
01:04:10.730 --> 01:04:11.434
10.

896
01:04:11.714 --> 01:04:13.997
Assuming the internal network is trusted.

897
01:04:14.277 --> 01:04:16.730
One compromised service then reaches all.

898
01:04:17.010 --> 01:04:20.999
Zero trust: mTLS and authZ on internal calls too.

899
01:04:21.279 --> 01:04:22.111
11.

900
01:04:22.391 --> 01:04:25.591
No unique IV, or a fast hash for passwords.

901
01:04:25.871 --> 01:04:34.234
Reusing a GCM IV under one key is catastrophic; SHA-256 for passwords is crackable.

902
01:04:34.514 --> 01:04:38.439
Unique IV per encryption, Argon2id for passwords.

903
01:04:39.789 --> 01:04:40.579
12.

904
01:04:40.859 --> 01:04:42.843
Verbose errors and enumeration.

905
01:04:43.123 --> 01:04:48.733
No such user versus wrong password leaks which accounts exist; stack traces leak internals.

906
01:04:49.013 --> 01:04:52.299
Return uniform errors; log details server-side.

907
