924
01:05:43.972 --> 01:05:53.529
A 6-digit code has a million possibilities, so a single guess has a 1-in-a-million chance, but with unlimited attempts, all million can be tried in minutes.

925
01:05:53.809 --> 01:06:01.831
A cap of about 5 attempts, code expiry, and a send limit make guessing infeasible (roughly 1 in 200,000 per code).

926
01:06:02.111 --> 01:06:06.377
L2: naive allowed unlimited guesses; hardened locked after 5.

927
01:06:07.727 --> 01:06:08.794
Question 4.

928
01:06:09.074 --> 01:06:10.674
What is envelope encryption?

929
01:06:10.954 --> 01:06:12.319
Thirty-second answer:

930
01:06:12.599 --> 01:06:22.135
Encrypt data with a per-record data key (DEK), then encrypt that DEK with a master key (KEK) that lives in a KMS and never leaves it.

931
01:06:22.415 --> 01:06:24.869
Store the ciphertext plus the wrapped DEK.

932
01:06:25.149 --> 01:06:28.093
To read, ask the KMS to unwrap the DEK.

933
01:06:28.373 --> 01:06:35.029
It means the master key never touches your servers, and rotating it only rewraps small keys, not all your data.

934
01:06:36.379 --> 01:06:37.381
Intermediate.

935
01:06:37.661 --> 01:06:38.771
Question 5.

936
01:06:39.051 --> 01:06:45.493
Explain OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code + PKCE and why PKCE matters.

937
01:06:45.773 --> 01:06:47.139
Thirty-second answer:

938
01:06:47.419 --> 01:07:03.099
The app redirects the user to the authorization server, which (after login) returns a one-time code to a registered redirect URI; the app exchanges the code plus a client secret (or, for public clients, PKCE) for tokens.

939
01:07:03.379 --> 01:07:11.080
PKCE: the app sends the hash of a random verifier up front (codechallenge), and presents the verifier at exchange.

940
01:07:11.360 --> 01:07:16.032
An intercepted code is useless without the verifier, which never left the device.

941
01:07:17.382 --> 01:07:18.491
Question 6.

942
01:07:18.771 --> 01:07:22.163
How do refresh tokens work, and what is reuse detection?

943
01:07:22.443 --> 01:07:23.809
Thirty-second answer:

944
01:07:24.089 --> 01:07:30.894
Access tokens are short-lived; a longer-lived refresh token gets new access tokens without re-login.

945
01:07:31.174 --> 01:07:36.507
With rotation, each refresh issues a new refresh token and invalidates the old one.

946
01:07:36.787 --> 01:07:47.305
If an already-used refresh token is presented again, that means someone has a stolen copy, reuse detection revokes the whole token family and forces re-login.

947
01:07:48.655 --> 01:07:49.743
Question 7.

948
01:07:50.023 --> 01:07:54.481
What is the OWASP API Security Top 10, and which matters most here?

949
01:07:54.761 --> 01:07:56.127
Thirty-second answer:

950
01:07:56.407 --> 01:07:59.329
A list of the most common API security risks.

951
01:07:59.609 --> 01:08:20.516
For a grocery platform the top ones are Broken Object Level Authorization (IDOR, API1), Broken Authentication (API2), Broken Object Property Level Authorization or mass assignment (API3), unrestricted resource consumption (API4), and SSRF (API7).

952
01:08:20.796 --> 01:08:23.889
API1 is consistently the most exploited.

953
01:08:25.239 --> 01:08:26.263
Question 8.

954
01:08:26.543 --> 01:08:29.381
RBAC versus ABAC: when do you need attributes?

955
01:08:29.661 --> 01:08:31.026
Thirty-second answer:

956
01:08:31.306 --> 01:08:38.431
RBAC maps roles to permissions, simple and auditable, but it can only say this role may do this action on this type.

957
01:08:38.711 --> 01:08:52.599
When you need conditions, only your own resource, only in your store, only up to 5,000, rupeesonly with an open ticket, you need ABAC, which evaluates attributes of the subject, resource, action and environment.

958
01:08:53.949 --> 01:08:55.016
Question 9.

959
01:08:55.296 --> 01:08:58.453
Where do secrets belong, and what are dynamic secrets?

960
01:08:58.733 --> 01:09:00.099
Thirty-second answer:

961
01:09:00.379 --> 01:09:08.571
In a secrets manager or KMS (Vault or OpenBao, cloud Secrets Manager), never in code, Git or plain env vars.

962
01:09:08.851 --> 01:09:25.448
Dynamic secrets go further: the KMS generates short-lived credentials on demand, for example a database username or password valid for an hour, unique to the service, and revokes them on expiry, so there's no shared long-lived password to leak.

963
01:09:26.798 --> 01:09:27.566
Senior.

964
01:09:27.846 --> 01:09:28.891
Question 10.

965
01:09:29.171 --> 01:09:32.051
Design authorization for the grocery platform.

966
01:09:32.331 --> 01:09:34.379
Which model(s), and how enforced?

967
01:09:34.659 --> 01:09:36.025
Thirty-second answer:

968
01:09:36.305 --> 01:09:37.222
Layer them:

969
01:09:37.502 --> 01:09:55.443
RBAC for coarse org roles (customer, rider, picker, store or city manager, finance), ABAC for conditions (ownership, store or city scope, refund limits, purpose), and ReBAC for object relationships and hierarchy (this order belongs to this store in this city).

970
01:09:55.723 --> 01:10:07.649
Centralize policy (OPA or Cedar + OpenFGA or SpiceDB), enforce locally in each service on every object, default deny, and test with a conformance suite.

971
01:10:08.999 --> 01:10:10.129
Question 11.

972
01:10:10.409 --> 01:10:16.233
How do you encrypt PII so a database breach is useless, and still be able to query and delete it?

973
01:10:16.513 --> 01:10:17.879
Thirty-second answer:

974
01:10:18.159 --> 01:10:28.079
Envelope-encrypt sensitive fields (AES-256-GCM + KMS-wrapped DEKs) so a DB or backup dump is ciphertext.

975
01:10:28.359 --> 01:10:39.452
For querying, keep searchable non-sensitive attributes in the clear or use deterministic encryption or blind indexes for equality lookups (with care, they leak equality).

976
01:10:39.732 --> 01:10:44.063
For deletion, use per-customer keys and crypto-shred (destroy the key).

977
01:10:45.413 --> 01:10:46.501
Question 12.

978
01:10:46.781 --> 01:10:49.618
Explain zero trust for service-to-service calls.

979
01:10:49.898 --> 01:10:51.263
Thirty-second answer:

980
01:10:51.543 --> 01:10:55.021
No service is trusted because of where it sits on the network.

981
01:10:55.301 --> 01:11:06.842
Each workload has a cryptographic identity (SPIFFE SVID), calls use mTLS, and the callee authorizes based on the caller's identity plus the propagated end-user context.

982
01:11:07.122 --> 01:11:17.149
Credentials are short-lived and auto-rotated, network policy is default-deny, and each service has least-privilege DB access with dynamic credentials.

983
01:11:18.499 --> 01:11:19.779
Question 13.

984
01:11:20.059 --> 01:11:23.515
A refund endpoint: walk through every control you'd apply.

985
01:11:24.865 --> 01:11:26.059
Question 14.

986
01:11:26.339 --> 01:11:30.990
How do you rotate a JWT signing key without breaking live tokens?

987
01:11:31.270 --> 01:11:32.635
Thirty-second answer:

988
01:11:32.915 --> 01:11:38.377
Publish the new key in the JWKS alongside the old one, each with a distinct kid.

989
01:11:38.657 --> 01:11:40.854
Start signing new tokens with the new key.

990
01:11:41.134 --> 01:11:48.025
Keep verifying with both until all tokens signed by the old key have expired (past the max token lifetime).

991
01:11:48.305 --> 01:11:49.755
Then remove the old key.

992
01:11:50.035 --> 01:11:55.006
Never rotate by replacing the current key, which invalidates every in-flight token.

993
01:11:56.356 --> 01:11:57.252
Architect.

994
01:11:57.532 --> 01:11:58.727
Question 15.

995
01:11:59.007 --> 01:12:03.636
Present a defense-in-depth architecture and threat model for the grocery platform.

996
01:12:03.916 --> 01:12:05.281
Thirty-second answer:

997
01:12:05.561 --> 01:12:07.353
Layers, each failing closed:

998
01:12:08.703 --> 01:12:09.898
Question 16.

999
01:12:10.178 --> 01:12:18.925
Design PII protection and 'delete my data' for DPDP or GDPR across DB, backups, logs and the data lake.

1000
01:12:20.275 --> 01:12:21.619
Question 17.

1001
01:12:21.899 --> 01:12:24.267
How do you secure the payment path end to end?

1002
01:12:24.547 --> 01:12:25.912
Thirty-second answer:

1003
01:12:26.192 --> 01:12:34.939
Don't handle raw card data, use a PCI-compliant gateway and tokenization, so cards never touch your servers (Ch 45).

1004
01:12:35.219 --> 01:12:36.179
For the flow:

1005
01:12:36.459 --> 01:12:55.147
TLS or mTLS throughout, strong authN, per-object authZ, idempotency keys (Ch 20), server-side amounts, signed webhooks verified on receipt, a tamper-evident ledger, envelope-encrypted stored payment metadata, fraud or anomaly checks, and full audit.

1006
01:12:55.427 --> 01:12:58.499
Scope PCI to the smallest possible surface.

1007
01:12:59.849 --> 01:13:01.022
Question 18.

1008
01:13:01.302 --> 01:13:04.779
Your authorization service is a new single point of failure.

1009
01:13:05.059 --> 01:13:07.555
How do you handle its availability and correctness?

1010
01:13:08.905 --> 01:13:10.143
Question 19.

1011
01:13:10.423 --> 01:13:14.092
What belongs in a security 'definition of done' and CI gate?

1012
01:13:15.442 --> 01:13:16.551
Question 20.

1013
01:13:16.831 --> 01:13:21.482
Where do you see AI or LLM features changing the security model here?

1014
01:13:21.762 --> 01:13:23.127
Thirty-second answer:

1015
01:13:23.407 --> 01:13:35.439
New surfaces: prompt injection (untrusted content steering an agent), excessive agency (an assistant with broad tool access), and sensitive-data leakage into prompts or logs.

1016
01:13:35.719 --> 01:13:55.538
Defenses reuse this chapter: the agent runs with least-privilege, per-user authZ (it can only do what the user could), tools enforce object-level checks, PII is minimized in prompts, outputs are treated as untrusted, and actions that move money or data need confirmation and are audited.

1017
01:13:56.888 --> 01:13:57.784
Part 9.

1018
01:13:58.064 --> 01:13:59.472
Summary and Practice.

1019
01:14:00.722 --> 01:14:01.810
Key takeaways.

1020
01:14:02.090 --> 01:14:05.973
AuthN proves who; authZ decides what, on every object.

1021
01:14:06.253 --> 01:14:08.493
Conflating them causes IDOR (L3).

1022
01:14:08.773 --> 01:14:16.986
Validate JWTs with a library: fix the algorithm, resolve keys by kid from a JWKS, check all claims.

1023
01:14:17.266 --> 01:14:21.298
Naive validation accepted 7 of 9 forgeries (L1).

1024
01:14:21.578 --> 01:14:22.751
Login controls:

1025
01:14:23.031 --> 01:14:31.714
OTP attempt or send limits and hashing (L2), OAuth Code+PKCE with registered redirects and single-use codes.

1026
01:14:31.994 --> 01:14:37.263
Short access tokens, rotating refresh tokens with reuse detection (L1 C:

1027
01:14:37.543 --> 01:14:41.618
43,200 versus 15 minutes of exposure).

1028
01:14:42.968 --> 01:14:44.355
Layer authorization:

1029
01:14:44.635 --> 01:14:51.739
RBAC + ABAC conditions + ReBAC relationships, default deny, enforced per object (L3).

1030
01:14:52.019 --> 01:14:58.355
Zero trust internally: mTLS, workload identity, dynamic least-privilege credentials.

1031
01:14:58.635 --> 01:15:03.307
Secrets in a KMS, dynamic where possible, never in code or images.

1032
01:15:03.587 --> 01:15:13.315
Envelope-encrypt PII (AES-256-GCM + KMS-wrapped DEKs); disk encryption alone is not enough (L4).

1033
01:15:13.595 --> 01:15:21.147
Rotation rewraps keys, not data; crypto-shredding erases across backups by destroying per-tenant keys (L4).

1034
01:15:22.497 --> 01:15:28.811
Make security testable: conformance suites (L1, L3) in CI, plus threat models and audits.

1035
01:15:29.091 --> 01:15:31.054
We think it's secure is not evidence.

